  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Dangerous heat and humidity expected Tuesday, \nwith heat indices over 100 degrees possible. Slightly less hot \nMonday and Wednesday with peak heat indices in the low to mid \n90s.\n\nLarge dome of high pressure will expand east across much of the \ncentral CONUS Monday, where its center will strengthen to an \nimpressive 600dam over the upper Mississippi Valley/Great Lakes. The \neastern periphery of this large ridge will then expand east into the \ncentral and lower Great Lakes region Tuesday. \n\nIn response, surface temperatures will rise above average Monday, \nhowever humidity levels will remain in check. This should keep heat \nindices mainly in the upper 80s to low 90s. Tuesday will be the \nhottest day with 850mb temps trending even a bit warmer with the \nlatest guidance, rising into the the 22C-24C range (99th percentile \nfor mid July), with the highest values across western NY. This will \nsupport highs in the low to mid 90s. When combined with rising \nhumidity levels, heat index values are expected to range from the \nmid 90s to low 100s across the lake plains, Genesee Valley, and \nFinger Lakes region, and low to mid 90s elsewhere.\n\nA fairly stout 30-40 knot LL jet will be located just 1-2 kft off \nthe deck Tuesday afternoon, allowing a good portion of those \nstronger winds aloft to mix to the surface. When combined with a \ntightening pressure gradient, expect to see frequent WSW to SW'erly \ngusts in the 25-35 mph range, with gusts to 40 mph possible \nnortheast of Lake Erie and east through northeast of Lake Ontario. \nThis will at least provide some natural relief to the heat, \nespecially across those areas downwind of the lakes.\n\nHot and humid conditions will remain for at least the first half of \nWednesday, before an approaching mid level trough and surface cold \nfront drop south out of eastern Canada and usher in cooler and drier \nair. High temps will still reach the upper 80s to low 90s by early \nWednesday afternoon across the south shore of Lake Ontario over to \nthe Finger Lakes, but dropping humidity levels will keep heat \nindices from climbing past the low to mid 90s.",
